{"id":"u-1ee285e2-8b2d-4d35-80ce-3e5c08f1d3cd","src":"community","title":"Shape & Strength: Women's Intermediate U/L","author":"Mithosphere","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/bD8wwq-shape-and-strength-womens-intermediate-ul","desc":"SHAPE & STRENGTH\nThis is designed specifically for intermediate female lifters who want to build serious strength and a powerful, shaped physique.\n\nMany women's programs make the mistake of completely ignoring the upper body, while standard powerlifting splits often lack the targeted lower-body volume that women actually want. This program bridges that gap. You will train your upper body with high-quality compound presses and rows to keep pace, while your lower-body days carry extra volume intentionally biased toward the glutes, hamstrings, and quads.\n\nYou will squat, hinge, press, and pull every single week, using structured progressive overload to consistently add weight to the bar.\n\nNote: This program is not for absolute beginners. If you have never used a barbell before, the total volume will be too high. Start with a basic full-body program first.\n\nPROGRESSION\nTo keep your strength climbing safely, the 10 weeks are broken down into distinct training blocks:\n- Weeks 1–3 BASELINE: Find your weights. Choose loads where you leave 2 solid reps in reserve (RIR 2) on every set. Log everything. Add weight only when you hit the top rep range on all sets.\n- Weeks 4–6 THE PUSH: Push the final set of your primary heavy compounds to 1 rep short of failure (RIR 1). Lower body volume increases slightly here to force adaptation.\n- Week 7 DELOAD: Do not skip this. Cut your total sets in half and use only 60% of your Week 6 weights. This gives your joints, tendons, and nervous system time to fully recover.\n- Weeks 8–10 PEAK: Return to your Week 6 loads—they will feel lighter. Take the final set of every movement to RIR 1. In Week 10, test your personal bests on your heavy barbell lifts.\n\nWARM-UP\n5-8 minutes of general work first, incline walking or rowing, just enough to break a light sweat. \n- Before lower days, run through worlds greatest stretch, cat cow stretch and a deep squat hold, then work up to your first heavy set with an empty bar and two or three progressively heavier ramp sets. \n- Before upper days, use band shoulder dislocate and thoracic spine rotation, then ramp the first press the same way. \nRamp sets are not working sets and you do not log them. Skipping the ramp on a heavy squat or a heavy hinge is how people find out what a tweaked back feels like.\n\nRECOVERY\nTake at least one full rest day between Lower A and Lower B, because your hamstrings and glutes are getting hit hard twice and they need the gap. \nIf a joint hurts sharply rather than just feeling worked, stop that exercise and see someone qualified. Soreness for two days after Lower A in week 1 is normal and it will fade by week 3.\n\nCARDIO\nWalk on off days. If you want extra cardio, keep it to two easy sessions a week and put them on non-lifting days or after lifting, never before a lower day.","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength","Women's"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":70,"joins":1,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-24T20:28:55.437Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Upper A","Lower A","Upper B","Lower B"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":6,"hi":8,"pct":75,"x":4}],"note":"Rest 2 minutes between sets."},{"n":"Single Arm Row (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":10,"rpe":8,"x":4}],"note":"Rest 90 seconds between sets."},{"n":"Seated Overhead Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":10,"rpe":8,"x":3}],"note":"Rest 90 seconds between sets."},{"n":"Seated Row (Cable)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":12,"rpe":8,"x":3}],"note":"Rest 75 seconds between sets."},{"n":"Lateral Raise (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":12,"hi":15,"rpe":8,"x":3}],"note":"Rest 45 seconds between sets."},{"n":"Tricep Pushdown (Cable)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":12,"rpe":8,"x":3}],"note":"Rest 45 seconds b